{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":6,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1568396006,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDI3NzkzOTI4OA=="},"target":"c145fae06b7bec8128be9271a033974ebfa2931a1372d3376a529b7aa41a4517","message":"In the world of \"I use datalad alone\" (no direct git/git-annex), we might need a command which would be likely just calling `git reset --hard [state]` but also (relates to our #1424) eventually handle submodules correctly.\n\nThe rationale is that `datalad` philosophy is to go between clean/saved states of the dataset.  But our commands can fail leaving repository in a not clean state. E.g. in our with @dnkennedy exploration of `datalad run` output was unlocked, but then command failed, and we were left with unlocked files on the drive preventing us to do any other `run`.  Resetting state to the prior clean state is the reasonable way to deal with such issues in a general systematic way.","files":null},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1568390396,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDI2MzI4MjI0MTE="},"added":["UX"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1568390407,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDI2MzI4MjI5NTE="},"added":["enhancement"],"removed":[]},{"type":2,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1568390413,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE3OlJlbmFtZWRUaXRsZUV2ZW50MjYzMjgyMzMyMw=="},"title":"[wishlist] datalad restore","was":"[wishlist] datalad restore"},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1568391683,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUzMTMwMjIxNQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3669#issuecomment-531302215"},"message":"\u003e In the world of \"I use datalad alone\" (no direct git/git-annex)\n\nI'm skeptical this world is anywhere close to existing or should.  Do we really not want to encourage use of at least the most basic operations, like `git log`?  But that's another topic.\n\n\u003e we might need a command which would be likely just calling `git reset --hard [state]` but also (relates to our \"states\"/branches manipulation issue TODO to find/link) eventually handle submodules correctly.\n\nI guess you're thinking of gh-1424.\n\n\u003e Resetting state to the prior clean state is the reasonable way to deal with such issues in a general systematic way.\n\nWouldn't this be wrapped up in the gh-1424 functionality?\n\n---\n\nA side note about a potential name clash worth keeping in mind: Git now has a `restore` command (essentially a combination of `reset`/`checkout` functionality for restoring files from some specified tree to the working tree or index).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1568396381,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUzMTMyNzU2MA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3669#issuecomment-531327560"},"message":"\u003e I guess you're thinking of [gh-1424](https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1424).\n\nTHANKS! adjusted\n\n\u003e \u003e Resetting state to the prior clean state is the reasonable way to deal with such issues in a general systematic way.\n\u003e \n\u003e Wouldn't this be wrapped up in the [gh-1424](https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1424) functionality?\n\nIt might!  But also it might be the one easiest to tackle to start with even without support of all the branch switching etc envisioned by that one IIRC.  It would be useful even without `-r` support, even if it would just fail if it detects that submodules are in \"incompatible\" state and require acting upon.\n\n\u003e A side note about a potential name clash worth keeping in mind: Git now has a `restore` command (essentially a combination of `reset`/`checkout` functionality for restoring files from some specified tree to the working tree or index).\n\nI might be the one who was never afraid of such clashes - IMHO it is pointless trying to come up with an \"alternative\" name for something of an existing clear semantic (note that do have already `clone`, `get`, `unlock`, `metadata` etc which exist in `git` and/or `git-annex` API). \n\nMoreover, the fact that git itself came up with `restore` as a user-level command for what was achievable with `checkout` points to the desire to have those two (switching state to some known other state - `checkout`) and restoring as two separate commands.","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1568397475,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDUzMTMzMjk3OA==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/3669#issuecomment-531332978"},"message":"\u003e Moreover, the fact that git itself came up with `restore` as a user-level command for what was achievable with `checkout` points to the desire to have those two (switching state to some known other state - `checkout`) and restoring as two separate commands.\n\nSure, the idea was that `git checkout` had grown to do too much, and it should be split up into a branch switching command (`git switch`) and a path restoring command (`git restore`).  But AFAIU the command from gh-1424 isn't equivalent to a `checkout`; it's more in line with the (non-pathspec version of) `git reset ...`.  And you're asking for a `git reset --hard REV`, so it would be 1424's \"load state\" with an additional flag to says \"discard my uncommitted changes\".","files":null},{"type":6,"author":{"id":"607ac2a9afabc0e44debe80e34283e413e009770"},"timestamp":1568397475,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDE1OlVzZXJDb250ZW50RWRpdDI3Nzk1MDMzNg=="},"target":"faf51685d6e0ae051984999565b78bece9ce68545f1d0866bf60c388658cebb3","message":"\u003e Moreover, the fact that git itself came up with `restore` as a user-level command for what was achievable with `checkout` points to the desire to have those two (switching state to some known other state - `checkout`) and restoring as two separate commands.\n\nSure, the idea was that `git checkout` had grown to do too much, and it should be split up into a branch switching command (`git switch`) and a path restoring command (`git restore`).  But AFAIU the command from gh-1424 isn't equivalent to a `checkout`; it's more in line with the (non-pathspec version of) `git reset ...`.  And you're asking for a `git reset --hard REV`, so it would be 1424's \"load state\" with an additional flag that says \"discard my uncommitted changes\".","files":null}]}